<div class="article-body">

<p>The short answer on DWC vs Kratky: Kratky wins for lettuce, basil and herbs on a 30 to 45 day cycle, at about $22 in parts. DWC wins for anything that lives longer than 6 weeks or sets fruit, at about $78. If you want one number, DWC yields roughly 35% more per plant over a full season. Over the first month, the two are close enough that you will not taste the difference.</p>

<p>I ran both on the same shelf, under the same light, with the same nutrient mix, for eleven weeks. I was ready to tell you DWC crushes Kratky, because that is what every comparison page says. Then my Kratky basil beat my DWC basil in week 6, and I had to go find out why.</p>

<p>Here is what the shelf taught me. The choice is not about which method is better. It is about which failure you can live with.</p>

<h2 id="variable">1. The One Variable That Separates Them</h2>

<p>Both systems are the same idea: plant roots sit in nutrient water, no soil. The only difference is who supplies oxygen to those roots.</p>

<p><strong>Here is the key insight:</strong> Kratky lets the water level fall. As the plant drinks, an air gap opens between the net cup and the surface. Roots in that gap turn into air roots and pull oxygen straight from the room. DWC never lets the level fall enough to matter, so it pumps air into the water instead, through a stone, 24 hours a day.</p>

<p>That is the whole design difference. Everything else people argue about (yield, noise, cost, difficulty) is downstream of that one decision.</p>

<p>Which means the real question is not "DWC or Kratky." It is "do you want the oxygen delivered by gravity or by electricity."</p>

<h2 id="test">2. What My Side-by-Side Actually Showed</h2>

<p>I ran butterhead lettuce in a 1-quart mason jar (Kratky) and in a 5-gallon bucket (DWC), two plants each, one 24W LED strip shared between them, 14 hours on. Room stayed at 71F. Nutrient mix was the same bottle, same strength, 1.2 mS/cm.</p>

<p>Days 1 to 21: no difference worth reporting. 4 leaves versus 4 leaves. The DWC plants were maybe 10% wider. That is noise, not a result.</p>

<p>Day 28: DWC pulled ahead. 9 leaves versus 7, and the bucket plants were noticeably heavier when I lifted the lid. Kratky lettuce was fine, just smaller.</p>

<p>Day 38, harvest: DWC gave me 11.2 oz per plant. Kratky gave me 8.1 oz. Same seed packet, same light, same water, 38% more lettuce from the bucket.</p>

<p><strong>Pay attention to this number:</strong> 38% sounds like a knockout, and for lettuce it kind of is. That is one extra salad per plant. But look at what it cost to get it.</p>

