<h2 id="rule">3. Kratky's One Rule You Cannot Break</h2>

<p>This is the part that ruins most first Kratky attempts, and almost no comparison article leads with it.</p>

<p>You cannot top off a Kratky jar. Not "should not." Cannot.</p>

<p>The air roots that formed in the gap above the waterline are not the same tissue as the water roots below it. They are built for air. If you fill the jar back up to the net cup because it looks low, you submerge them. They drown, then rot, and the plant that was doing fine last week collapses in four days.</p>

<p>I did this to my first jar in March. The water looked scary-low, so I topped it up like a houseplant. The basil sulked for two days, then went limp, then smelled like a swamp. The fix is simple and it feels wrong: let it run low. That is the method working, not failing.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <strong>Kratky top-off rule:</strong> start the jar at full strength, then leave it alone until harvest. If the level drops below about a third of the jar, the cycle is over. Start a new jar instead of rescuing the old one. A wide-mouth quart jar feeds one lettuce or basil plant for roughly 5 weeks.
</div>

<p>If you want the jar build itself, step by step, the <a href="/posts/kratky-method-for-beginners">Kratky method for beginners</a> covers it. That article is the setup. This one is the decision.</p>

<h2 id="dwc">4. Where DWC Wins</h2>

<p>Anything that lives past 6 weeks. Peppers, cherry tomatoes, strawberries, a basil plant you keep cutting for four months. Kratky runs out of reservoir, and when it does you have no move, because the one move you have kills the plant.</p>

<p>DWC also wins on rescue. When something goes wrong in a bucket, you can dump half the water, refill, adjust pH, and the plant keeps its oxygen the whole time because the pump never stopped. In a jar, every correction is a gamble on the air gap.</p>

<p>And DWC is the friendlier system if you like measuring. You can check EC and pH without disturbing anything. In a jar, sticking a probe in means pushing it past roots that are trying to stay dry.</p>

<p>If you are going the bucket route, a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=5+gallon+DWC+hydroponic+bucket+kit&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">5-gallon DWC bucket kit</a> with a lid and net pot is the sane starting point, plus a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=quiet+aquarium+air+pump+air+stone+kit&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">quiet air pump and air stone kit</a>. Buy the pump one size bigger than the bucket needs. A cheap pump that barely bubbles is the most common reason a bucket underperforms a jar.</p>

<h2 id="kratky">5. Where Kratky Wins</h2>

<p>Short crops, small spaces, and anyone who does not want another appliance humming in the kitchen.</p>

<p>My honest ranking for a total beginner: Kratky first, every time. Not because it yields more, but because it teaches you the two things that actually decide success (reservoir volume and nutrient strength) with no pump to hide behind. When your jar works, you know it was you.</p>

<p>Kratky also survives things DWC does not. A three-day power cut, a vacation, a cat. Nothing to unplug means nothing to fail.</p>

<p>The parts list is almost insulting: a wide-mouth mason jar, a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=mason+jar+net+cup+lid+hydroponic&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">net cup lid</a>, some <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=expanded+clay+pebbles+hydroton&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">clay pebbles</a>, a rockwool cube, and one bottle of nutrients. That is $22 if you already own a jar.</p>

<p>One warning that saves a lot of grief: wrap the jar in foil or paint it. Clear glass plus nutrient water plus a grow light equals algae in about ten days. That is covered in detail in the <a href="/posts/how-to-clean-hydroponic-algae">algae cleanup guide</a>, but the short version is block the light before it starts.</p>

<h2 id="hidden">6. The Hidden Cost Missing From Every Table</h2>

<p>This is where it gets interesting. The pump does two things nobody lists: it moves air, and it adds heat.</p>

<p>My cheap 4W pump ran my bucket 6F warmer than the jar sitting next to it, measured with an aquarium thermometer at 4pm. 71F room, 77F bucket. That is not a rounding error. Warm water holds less dissolved oxygen, which is the exact thing the pump is supposed to be delivering.</p>

<p>Then in July the room hit 78F and the bucket went to 83F. The DWC lettuce stalled while the jar, which had less water but no pump, sat at 80F and kept growing. My pump had been working against itself for two weeks. If you want the numbers behind that, they are in the <a href="/posts/hydroponic-water-temperature">water temperature guide</a>.</p>

<p>The other hidden cost is pH drift. An air stone strips CO2 out of the water, and CO2 is what makes water slightly acidic. So a bubbling bucket climbs in pH, slowly, every day. Mine went from 5.9 to 6.6 in four days with no plants even drinking. You end up adjusting pH more often than a jar does, which is the opposite of what the comparison tables promise.</p>

<p>None of this makes DWC bad. It makes DWC a system that needs a thermometer, not just a pump.</p>

<h2 id="honest">7. Here Is the Honest Truth</h2>

<p><strong>Here is the honest truth</strong> about Kratky: the yield claim people make for it is oversold. A jar is not a production system. Two lettuce plants per jar is the ceiling, and if you want salad twice a week you need four jars running on staggered starts, which is more shelf space than one bucket.</p>

<p>Kratky is also terrible at recovery. When a jar goes wrong you usually find out too late, because you cannot see the roots without lifting the lid and destroying the air gap. DWC lets you look. That visibility is worth real money the first time something starts to rot.</p>

<p>For me, the split that stuck: jars for greens and herbs I harvest within five weeks, one bucket for the pepper plant that has to survive until December. Two systems, not one winner.</p>

<h2 id="pick">8. Which One Should You Build</h2>

<p>If you have never grown anything hydroponically, build a Kratky jar this week. Total cost under $25, no electricity, and it will teach you more in one cycle than a month of reading.</p>

<p>If you already have one harvest behind you, or you want peppers and tomatoes, add a bucket. Not instead. In addition.</p>

<p>A starter kit is fine too if you would rather skip the parts hunt. My <a href="/posts/best-hydroponic-system-for-beginners">beginner system roundup</a> covers which kits are actually sized right, and the <a href="/posts/hydroponic-growing-medium">growing medium comparison</a> answers what goes in the net cup.</p>

<p>Whichever you pick, do not skip the meter. A <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=digital+pH+meter+hydroponics&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">digital pH meter</a> at around $16 and an <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=EC+TDS+meter+hydroponics&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">EC and TDS meter</a> at around $14 do more for yield than the upgrade from jar to bucket. Both drift, so grab <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=pH+calibration+solution+4.0+7.0&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">calibration solution</a> while you are there. It is $10 and it is the difference between a reading and a guess.</p>

<p><strong>Your next move:</strong> pick your crop first, then your method. Under 45 days, go jar. Over 60, go bucket. Anything in between, go jar and start planning the bucket. And if you are buying the bucket, price bounces between $55 and $95. Under $70 is a solid deal.</p>

